  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These are the <b>techniques</b> that show up in harder problems. Each one builds on what you already know.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-the-problem-nbsp",
      "title": "1. The Problem&nbsp;",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Say you are merging two sorted lists:</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247584354-407232190.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247584354-407232190.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247584354-407232190.png",
          "alt": "Two sorted linked lists before merging"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247274051-206922000.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247274051-206922000.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247274051-206922000.png",
          "alt": "Comparing list heads to decide the merged head"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You start building the merged list. But immediately you run into a problem:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "When merging two sorted linked lists, the first question is:&nbsp;<strong>which node should be the head of the new list?</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You cannot decide this beforehand you must&nbsp;<strong>compare the first elements of both lists</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "If the first node of&nbsp;<strong>List1 is smaller</strong>, then it becomes the head",
            "If the first node of&nbsp;<strong>List2 is smaller</strong>, then it becomes the head"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "text": "<p><b>Example idea:</b></p><p>You always pick the&nbsp;<strong>smaller value first</strong>, because the final list must remain&nbsp;<strong>sorted</strong>.</p>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-dummy-node-technique",
      "title": "2. Dummy Node Technique",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A fake placeholder node inserted&nbsp;<strong>before the real head</strong>. Gives you a fixed anchor so you never have to write special case logic for the head.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247275524-864687939.png\" alt=\"mermaid-diagram-112.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Without a dummy node, the head becomes a special case, so you need extra conditions and the code gets messy.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "With a dummy node, all nodes are treated the same, so you can write a clean and simple loop without special handling.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247276966-220834829.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247276966-220834829.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247276966-220834829.png",
          "alt": "Building the merged list using dummy and tail pointers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node dummy = new Node(0);\nNode tail = dummy;\n\n// keep appending to tail\ntail.next = newNode;\ntail = tail.next;\n\nreturn dummy.next;    // skip dummy, return real head"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Use it whenever you are&nbsp;<strong>building a new </strong><b>listor</b> the&nbsp;<strong>head might change</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-sub-list-reversal",
      "title": "3. Sub-List Reversal",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You only reverse the part of the list<strong>between L and R</strong>, not the whole list.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Reverse only:<code>20 → 30 → 40</code>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You have a linked list, and you only want to reverse the part between positions&nbsp;<strong>L and R</strong>.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247278385-644175798.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247278385-644175798.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247278385-644175798.png",
          "alt": "Sub-list bounded by positions L and R"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "First, go to position&nbsp;<strong>L</strong>&nbsp;and keep track of the node just before it (<code>prevNode</code>).&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The node at L is the start of the sub-list (<code>subHead</code>). Then go to position&nbsp;<strong>R</strong>, where&nbsp;<code>subTail</code>&nbsp;is, and also note the node after it (<code>nextNode</code>).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now reverse only the nodes between L and R. After reversing, the start becomes the end and the end becomes the start. So&nbsp;<code>subTail</code>&nbsp;becomes the new front, and&nbsp;<code>subHead</code>&nbsp;becomes the new end.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247279932-645162967.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally, reconnect everything: connect&nbsp;<code>prevNode</code>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<code>subTail</code>, and connect&nbsp;<code>subHead</code>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<code>nextNode</code>.&nbsp;<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><b>Done</b>&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "// walk to node before L\nNode prevNode = dummy;\nfor (int i = 0; i < L - 1; i++) prevNode = prevNode.next;\n\n// reverse the sub-list\nNode subHead = prevNode.next;\nNode prev = null, curr = subHead;\nfor (int i = 0; i <= R - L; i++) {\n    Node next = curr.next;\n    curr.next = prev;\n    prev = curr;\n    curr = next;\n}\n\n// reconnect\nprevNode.next = prev;       // prev is new front of sub-list\nsubHead.next  = curr;       // curr is node after sub-list"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-k-group-reversal",
      "title": "3. K-Group Reversal",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Think of this as breaking the linked list into&nbsp;<strong>groups of size K</strong>&nbsp;and reversing each group.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For example, with&nbsp;<strong>K = 3</strong>, the list becomes:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247281397-238282828.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247281397-238282828.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247281397-238282828.png",
          "alt": "Linked list split into groups of size K"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now, reverse only the full groups:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247282820-669414663.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The last group<code>[7]</code>is smaller than K, so it stays the same.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node groupPrev = dummy;\n\nwhile (true) {\n    Node kth = getKth(groupPrev, k);\n    if (kth == null) break;              // less than K nodes left\n\n    Node groupNext = kth.next;\n    Node subHead   = groupPrev.next;\n\n    // reverse K nodes\n    Node prev = groupNext, curr = subHead;\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {\n        Node next = curr.next;\n        curr.next = prev;\n        prev = curr;\n        curr = next;\n    }\n\n    groupPrev.next = kth;                // reconnect left side\n    groupPrev      = subHead;            // subHead is now group tail\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-removing-duplicates",
      "title": "4. Removing Duplicates",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There are two common variants, depending on what the problem asks.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Variant A: &nbsp;Keep One Copy",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Here, you remove duplicates but&nbsp;<strong>keep one occurrence</strong>&nbsp;of each value.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247284297-470557681.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247284297-470557681.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247284297-470557681.png",
          "alt": "Sorted list with duplicate values before dedup"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea is simple: traverse the list, and whenever the current node and next node have the same value, skip the next node.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247285837-966747778.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This way, only one copy of each element remains.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node ptr = head;\nwhile (ptr != null && ptr.next != null) {\n    if (ptr.val == ptr.next.val)\n        ptr.next = ptr.next.next;   // skip duplicate\n    else\n        ptr = ptr.next;\n}"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Variant B: Remove All Occurrences",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Here, you remove&nbsp;<strong>all nodes that have duplicates</strong>, not just extra copies.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247287331-787454280.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea is: if a value appears more than once,&nbsp;<strong>remove it completely</strong>&nbsp;from the list.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247288726-381386324.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So while traversing, whenever you see duplicate values (like&nbsp;<code>2 → 2</code>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<code>3 → 3</code>), skip&nbsp;<strong>all nodes with that value</strong>, not just one. Only nodes with unique values remain&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node dummy = new Node(0);\ndummy.next = head;\nNode prev = dummy;\n\nwhile (prev.next != null) {\n    Node curr = prev.next;\n    if (curr.next != null && curr.val == curr.next.val) {\n        int dupVal = curr.val;\n        while (prev.next != null && prev.next.val == dupVal)\n            prev.next = prev.next.next;   // skip entire group\n    } else {\n        prev = prev.next;\n    }\n}\nreturn dummy.next;"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-flattening-a-multilevel-list",
      "title": "5. Flattening a Multilevel List",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In this problem, each node has two pointers:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<code>next</code>&nbsp;→ normal right pointer",
            "<code>child</code>&nbsp;→ points to another list (downwards)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Your goal is to convert everything into a&nbsp;<strong>single-level list</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247290151-788615988.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea is simple: whenever you see a node with a&nbsp;<strong>child</strong>, insert that child list&nbsp;<strong>in between</strong>&nbsp;the node and its next.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So at node&nbsp;<code>2</code>, you take its child list (<code>4 → 5 → 6</code>) and place it right after&nbsp;<code>2</code>. Then connect the end of that child list back to&nbsp;<code>3</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247291792-352944606.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "In short:&nbsp;<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">go depth-first:&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">process child first, then continue the original next.</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Whenever you hit a node with a child insert the entire child list between current and current.next.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "Node curr = head;\nwhile (curr != null) {\n    if (curr.child != null) {\n        Node child     = curr.child;\n        Node savedNext = curr.next;\n\n        curr.next  = child;\n        curr.child = null;\n\n        // find tail of child list\n        Node tail = child;\n        while (tail.next != null) tail = tail.next;\n\n        tail.next = savedNext;    // connect child tail to saved next\n    }\n    curr = curr.next;\n}\nreturn head;"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-reorder-list",
      "title": "6. Reorder List",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Rearrange the list so nodes come in this order:<br><strong>first → last → second → second-last → ...</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><br></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247293303-751385132.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The idea is done in&nbsp;<strong>3 simple steps</strong>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "First, find the&nbsp;<strong>middle of the list</strong>&nbsp;using slow and fast pointers. This splits the list into two halves.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Second,&nbsp;<strong>reverse the second half</strong>&nbsp;of the list. So&nbsp;<code>4 → 5</code>&nbsp;becomes&nbsp;<code>5 → 4</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247294798-328373886.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247294798-328373886.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785247294798-328373886.png",
          "alt": "Second half of the list reversed"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Finally,&nbsp;<strong>merge the two halves alternately</strong>: take one node from the first half, then one from the reversed second half, and keep going.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So it becomes:<br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785247296235-292465194.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": [],
          "content": null
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "// Step 1 — find middle\nNode slow = head, fast = head;\nwhile (fast != null && fast.next != null) {\n    slow = slow.next;\n    fast = fast.next.next;\n}\n\n// Step 2 — reverse second half\nNode prev = null, curr = slow.next;\nslow.next = null;\nwhile (curr != null) {\n    Node next = curr.next;\n    curr.next = prev;\n    prev = curr;\n    curr = next;\n}\n\n// Step 3 — interleave\nNode p1 = head, p2 = prev;\nwhile (p2 != null) {\n    Node next1 = p1.next, next2 = p2.next;\n    p1.next = p2;\n    p2.next = next1;\n    p1 = next1;\n    p2 = next2;\n}"
          }
        },
